1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:05,545
Balkh, the year one thousand and thirty two. A
gallows stands outside the city. The

2
00:00:05,545 --> 00:00:10,175
man they bring to it was, not long ago, the
most powerful minister in the empire.

3
00:00:10,175 --> 00:00:14,634
And when it was over, he was left alone, just
as alone as he had come from his

4
00:00:14,634 --> 00:00:15,435
mother's womb.

5
00:00:25,785 --> 00:00:30,477
Welcome to The Honest Historian. I'm Mehdi
Rahmani, and this is Folio One. Every

6
00:00:30,477 --> 00:00:35,286
week, I open a book that was written nearly a
thousand years ago, in Persian, by a

7
00:00:35,286 --> 00:00:40,154
man who made his readers a promise. He
promised to write only what he had seen with

8
00:00:40,154 --> 00:00:42,851
his own eyes, or heard from people he trusted.

9
00:00:43,131 --> 00:00:48,000
His name was Abolfazl Beyhaqi, and today, I
want to tell you the story that made him

10
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:49,489
famous.

11
00:00:50,269 --> 00:00:55,655
It is the story of a vizier named Hasanak. A
powerful man, a proud man, and, in the

12
00:00:55,655 --> 00:00:59,549
end, a man standing alone on a gallows in the
city of Balkh.

13
00:01:14,829 --> 00:01:19,548
Let me take you to the beginning of the
eleventh century. In the east of the Persian

14
00:01:19,548 --> 00:01:24,268
world, in what is today Afghanistan, there was
a great empire ruled from the city of

15
00:01:24,268 --> 00:01:28,875
Ghazni. Its king was Sultan Mahmud of Ghazni,
one of the most famous rulers of his

16
00:01:28,875 --> 00:01:32,471
age. His armies marched as far as India. Poets
filled his court.

17
00:01:32,751 --> 00:01:38,163
And next to him, managing the endless business
of the empire, stood his vizier, his

18
00:01:38,163 --> 00:01:39,727
chief minister: Hasanak.

19
00:01:40,507 --> 00:01:45,093
Hasanak came from a respected family of
Nishapur. He was young for such a high

20
00:01:45,093 --> 00:01:49,973
position, clever, wealthy, and very sure of
himself. When you are the right hand of

21
00:01:49,973 --> 00:01:54,911
the most powerful king in the region, it is
easy to believe the world will always be

22
00:01:54,911 --> 00:01:55,675
on your side.

23
00:01:56,455 --> 00:02:01,095
But Sultan Mahmud had two sons. One was
Muhammad, the son he favored. The other was

24
00:02:01,095 --> 00:02:05,678
Masud, a strong and ambitious prince who did
not always get along with his father.

25
00:02:05,678 --> 00:02:10,206
And Hasanak made a choice that seemed safe at
the time. He stood with the father,

26
00:02:10,206 --> 00:02:13,671
and with Muhammad, and he did not hide his
contempt for Masud.

27
00:02:14,451 --> 00:02:19,545
Beyhaqi tells us that Hasanak once said
something sharp and insulting about Masud,

28
00:02:19,545 --> 00:02:24,577
words along the lines of: if Masud ever
becomes king, let them hang me. People at

29
00:02:24,577 --> 00:02:27,870
court remembered it. People at court always
remember.

30
00:02:43,150 --> 00:02:47,705
There was a second problem. Years earlier,
Hasanak had gone on the pilgrimage to

31
00:02:47,705 --> 00:02:52,316
Mecca. On the way back, he did not return the
usual way. He passed through Egypt,

32
00:02:52,316 --> 00:02:56,928
which was ruled by the Fatimid caliphs, the
great rivals of the Abbasid caliph in

33
00:02:56,928 --> 00:03:01,198
Baghdad. In Egypt, Hasanak accepted a robe of
honor from the Fatimid court.

34
00:03:01,978 --> 00:03:06,672
To us, that may sound like a simple gift. But
in that world, it was politics. The

35
00:03:06,672 --> 00:03:11,307
caliph in Baghdad was furious. To him,
accepting honors from the Fatimids looked

36
00:03:11,307 --> 00:03:15,885
like loyalty to his enemies, and his letters
began to call Hasanak a heretic, a

37
00:03:15,885 --> 00:03:19,535
Qarmatian, a word that in those days could
cost a man his life.

38
00:03:19,815 --> 00:03:25,374
Sultan Mahmud, it is said, protected his
vizier and brushed the accusations aside.

39
00:03:25,374 --> 00:03:28,221
As long as Mahmud lived, Hasanak was safe.

40
00:03:29,001 --> 00:03:33,246
And then, in the year 1030, Sultan Mahmud
died.

41
00:03:48,526 --> 00:03:53,194
What followed was a short and bitter struggle
between the brothers. Muhammad was

42
00:03:53,194 --> 00:03:57,570
placed on the throne first, but he did not
last. Masud, the son Hasanak had

43
00:03:57,570 --> 00:04:02,413
insulted, won. He became Sultan. And suddenly,
everything that Hasanak had said and

44
00:04:02,413 --> 00:04:04,163
done came back to the surface.

45
00:04:04,943 --> 00:04:09,547
Hasanak was arrested. And at court there was a
man who had been waiting for this

46
00:04:09,547 --> 00:04:14,037
moment for a long time. His name was Bu Sahl
Zuzani. Beyhaqi does not hide his

47
00:04:14,037 --> 00:04:18,526
opinion of him. He describes Bu Sahl as a man
with a cruel streak, someone who

48
00:04:18,526 --> 00:04:22,842
enjoyed watching great men fall, and who had
an old grudge against Hasanak.

49
00:04:23,622 --> 00:04:28,687
Bu Sahl pressed the new Sultan again and
again. The caliph in Baghdad wants this man

50
00:04:28,687 --> 00:04:33,330
punished, he said. He is a heretic. He
insulted you. Masud, Beyhaqi suggests,

51
00:04:33,330 --> 00:04:38,275
hesitated. But the pressure, the letters from
Baghdad, and the old insult were too

52
00:04:38,275 --> 00:04:38,577
much.

53
00:04:53,857 --> 00:04:58,235
Before the end, there was a hearing. Hasanak
was brought before the new chief

54
00:04:58,235 --> 00:05:02,898
minister, an old and experienced statesman
named Ahmad Hasan Maymandi. Hasanak had

55
00:05:02,898 --> 00:05:07,675
lost everything: his office, his wealth, his
freedom. Beyhaqi describes him arriving

56
00:05:07,675 --> 00:05:11,030
in plain clothes, a man who had once dressed
like a prince.

57
00:05:11,810 --> 00:05:16,343
And here Beyhaqi gives us one of the most
human scenes in the whole book. When

58
00:05:16,343 --> 00:05:20,702
Hasanak was brought in, the old minister
treated him with respect. Not as a

59
00:05:20,702 --> 00:05:25,351
prisoner, but as a man who had once held the
same office. Bu Sahl, watching, was

60
00:05:25,351 --> 00:05:28,663
furious and began to insult Hasanak in front
of everyone.

61
00:05:28,943 --> 00:05:34,249
And the old minister turned on Bu Sahl and
silenced him. Whatever this man has done,

62
00:05:34,249 --> 00:05:38,607
he was saying, you do not humiliate a fallen
man. Not in my presence.

63
00:05:39,387 --> 00:05:44,727
Hasanak, for his part, did not beg. He
answered calmly and with dignity. He knew how

64
00:05:44,727 --> 00:05:45,681
this would end.

65
00:06:00,961 --> 00:06:05,988
The execution took place in the city of Balkh,
in the year 1032. A gallows was set

66
00:06:05,988 --> 00:06:10,771
up near the city. Hasanak was led out, his
head covered. Beyhaqi tells us that

67
00:06:10,771 --> 00:06:15,860
people had been paid to throw stones at him,
as was the custom with those condemned

68
00:06:15,860 --> 00:06:18,006
as heretics. But almost no one did.

69
00:06:18,286 --> 00:06:21,507
The crowd stood there, and many of them wept.

70
00:06:22,287 --> 00:06:27,328
And then Beyhaqi writes one of the most famous
lines in Persian prose. I'll say it

71
00:06:27,328 --> 00:06:32,246
in Persian, and then in English. Hasanak tanha
mand, chenanke tanha amade bud az

72
00:06:32,246 --> 00:06:36,979
shekam-e madar. Hasanak was left alone, just
as alone as he had come from his

73
00:06:36,979 --> 00:06:37,839
mother's womb.

74
00:06:38,619 --> 00:06:43,462
His body, Beyhaqi says, remained on the
gallows for about seven years.

75
00:06:58,742 --> 00:07:03,955
There is one more person in this story, and
for many readers she is the true hero.

76
00:07:03,955 --> 00:07:05,035
Hasanak's mother.

77
00:07:05,815 --> 00:07:10,123
When the news was brought to her, the people
around her expected screaming,

78
00:07:10,123 --> 00:07:14,890
collapse, curses. Instead, she was silent for
a moment, and then she said something

79
00:07:14,890 --> 00:07:19,543
that Persian speakers still quote a thousand
years later. In Beyhaqi's words, she

80
00:07:19,543 --> 00:07:21,495
said: what a great man my son was.

81
00:07:21,775 --> 00:07:27,109
A king like Mahmud gave him this world, and a
king like Masud gave him the next.

82
00:07:27,889 --> 00:07:32,680
Think about that. In one sentence, she refused
to let the powerful decide what her

83
00:07:32,680 --> 00:07:37,062
son's life meant. They had taken his body.
They would not take his dignity.

84
00:07:52,342 --> 00:07:57,678
So why did Beyhaqi, a young secretary who
worked for this same Sultan Masud, write

85
00:07:57,678 --> 00:07:58,785
all of this down?

86
00:07:59,565 --> 00:08:04,338
That is the part I love most. Beyhaqi wrote
his history years later, after Masud

87
00:08:04,338 --> 00:08:09,231
himself was gone. He could have flattered the
dynasty he served. Instead, he wrote

88
00:08:09,231 --> 00:08:13,945
that Hasanak's death was unjust, that Bu Sahl
was driven by spite, and that the

89
00:08:13,945 --> 00:08:15,138
crowd in Balkh wept.

90
00:08:15,418 --> 00:08:19,992
He even tells us, more or less, why he is
telling us: so that those who read this

91
00:08:19,992 --> 00:08:24,284
will learn that power does not last, that the
world turns, and that a man is

92
00:08:24,284 --> 00:08:27,898
remembered for how he behaves when everything
is taken from him.

93
00:08:28,678 --> 00:08:33,427
That is why I call him The Honest Historian.
In an age when most chroniclers wrote

94
00:08:33,427 --> 00:08:37,771
to please kings, Beyhaqi wrote to tell the
truth. And he gave us, almost by

95
00:08:37,771 --> 00:08:41,478
accident, some of the most beautiful Persian
prose ever written.

96
00:08:56,758 --> 00:09:01,902
Next week, we'll stay at the court of Ghazni
and meet the new Sultan, Masud himself:

97
00:09:01,902 --> 00:09:06,801
a brave, impulsive, complicated king, and the
long road that took him from rebel

98
00:09:06,801 --> 00:09:08,577
prince to ruler of an empire.

99
00:09:09,357 --> 00:09:14,110
If you enjoyed this story, follow the show so
you don't miss the next one, and share

100
00:09:14,110 --> 00:09:18,751
it with someone who loves history. I'm Mehdi
Rahmani. This episode was retold from

101
00:09:18,751 --> 00:09:23,505
the Persian original of the History of
Beyhaqi, and narrated with an AI rendering of

102
00:09:23,505 --> 00:09:25,655
my own voice. Thank you for listening.

